Which biomes grow which tree is set in `data/mysticalterrain/grand_trees.json`, which lists for every biome the features from `worldgen/placed_feature/grand_tree/` it grows. A grown species has one placed feature and grows at the rate `grand_tree_rates.json` gives it in each biome (trees per chunk; below 1 it's the chance per chunk); the older trees have a placed feature per rate. Landmark and colossus spacing is set in `worldgen/structure_set/`.

## The End and the Nether

The End gets 12 biomes and the Nether 6, built mostly from vanilla blocks, with starstone and ashstone outcrops of the mod's own. The End is where most of the work went: every biome has its own ground, its own features and at least one structure. The main End island is left alone.

In the End, TerraBlender splits the outer islands into four zones (the high middles of the big islands, their slopes, their edges, and the scattered islets over the void) and each biome grows in one of them. Each has a weight of 4 or 6 against vanilla's 10 for its zone, so roughly three fifths of the outer End is new. End cities still generate in the new highland and midland biomes. The Nether biomes fill a TerraBlender region of their own, weight 7 against vanilla's 10, and fortresses, bastions and ruined portals still turn up in them.

| End biome | Zone | What's there |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Amethyst Spires | Highlands | Clusters of giant purple crystal spires up to 120 blocks tall, smaller clusters and fallen shards, amethyst-veined ground covered in buds. Crystal Sanctums hide a chest under a floating crystal. |
| Chorus Wilds | Highlands | Dense chorus, Chorus Titans and Chorus Parasols (purpur trunks with glassy canopies and glowing fruit) and purpur ruins. |
| Weeping Peaks | Highlands | Blackstone and obsidian ground, crying obsidian spires, purple tears falling, and Obsidian Circles of monoliths around an altar. |
| Starfall Craters | Highlands | Meteor craters from small pits to great ones 50 blocks across with the meteorite, iron and all, still lying in the bottom. |
| Pearlbloom Glades | Midlands | Pale moss under pink-leaved pale oaks hung with moss and froglights, petals, lilies and eyeblossoms. |
| Frostvoid Expanse | Midlands | Snow and packed ice, ice spikes, trees of ice, and glacier crystals up to 90 blocks tall. |
| Void Reef | Midlands | Warped nylium and dark prismarine, and reefs of branching prismarine tipped with sea lanterns. |
| Echoing Hollows | Midlands | Deepslate and sculk, deepslate pillars and Echo Gates with a soul lantern under the keystone. |
| Shattered Rim | Edge | Broken crystals, toppled purpur columns and boulders along the island edges, and purpur ruins. |
| Leviathan Barrens | Edge | Bone-strewn ground and the skeletons of void leviathans, the biggest over 60 blocks long. |
| Nebula Drift | Islets | Little glowing stars and floating crystals hanging in the void, and great Nebula Hearts. |
| Geode Archipelago | Islets | Floating islands with glowing drips, bigger sky isles, and floating amethyst geodes cracked open. |

| Nether biome | What's there |
| --- | --- |
| Emberwood Forest | Crimson nylium under Emberwoods: gnarled crimson trees with magma veins, shroomlights and weeping vines. |
| Soulwood Thicket | Soul soil and soul sand, weeping Soulwoods with warped canopies, soul fire. |
| Quartz Caverns | Quartz crystal clusters on the floor and quartz stalactites overhead, extra quartz and glowstone. |
| Gilded Ruins | Blackstone and gilded blackstone, ruined blackstone halls and Piglin Shrines with chests, extra gold. |
| Bone Wastes | Soul soil and gravel, fossils, withered bone trees and the skeletons of nether leviathans. |
| Magma Rifts | Basalt and magma, craters and magma spires. |

The structures, for `/locate structure mysticalterrain:<id>`: `great_amethyst_spire`, `crystal_sanctum`, `purpur_ruins`, `obsidian_circle`, `great_crater`, `glacier_crystal`, `great_reef`, `echo_gate`, `void_leviathan`, `nebula_heart`, `sky_isle` and `great_geode` in the End; `gilded_ruin`, `piglin_shrine` and `nether_leviathan` in the Nether. The shrines, circles and ruins have a chest with End city or bastion loot.

The End biomes are handed out by vanilla's End biome source, so they also show up alongside End terrain mods that keep it and only reshape the islands.

## Blocks

200 new blocks, in their own creative tab and in vanilla's Building Blocks and Natural Blocks tabs (with a lot of mods installed, the creative inventory moves mod tabs onto later pages; use the arrows at the top of the inventory to reach them). Their textures are 32x and drawn in a realistic style to sit next to packs like Patrix, and every one has labPBR normal and specular maps, so shaders give bark, stone and leaves real depth and let embers, runes, stars and glowcaps glow.

**16x toggle.** The mod also ships every block and item texture at 16x, for vanilla-resolution packs. Press **F7** in game (rebindable under Controls, Mystical Terrain) to switch between 16x and 32x, or turn on the "Mystical Terrain 16x" pack in Options, Resource Packs. It covers everything, doors, leaves and creative tab icons included, and has its own 16x normal and specular maps. Log tops, bricks, doors and trapdoors are laid out again on the 16 grid; the rest are scaled down with a filter that keeps cracks and edges sharp.

- **12 wood sets**, one per realm, each with a log, wood, stripped log and wood, planks, stairs, slab, fence, fence gate, door and trapdoor: Mystwood, Maple, Rimewood, Charwood, Silverbark, Ginkgo, Gloamwood, Palm, Cypress, Olive, Sporewood and Starbark. Logs strip with an axe, and planks, sticks, doors and the rest craft like vanilla's.
- **15 leaves**: mystwood, starbloom blossoms, maple, golden maple, rimewood, ember (glowing), silverbark, wisteria, ginkgo, gloamwood, palm fronds, mango, cypress, olive and starbark. Plus sporecap and glowcap blocks for the fungal realm's mushrooms.
- **10 stones and their bricks**: runestone, russet granite, rimestone, ashstone, moonstone, fossil stone, gloomstone, mirestone, sandrock and starstone. Four stone make four bricks, or use a stonecutter.
- **9 soils**: leaf mold, permafrost, ash soil, fae moss, primal moss, gloam moss, peat, sunbaked clay and spore soil. They count as dirt, so plants and trees grow on them.
- **22 plants**: goldenrod, snowbell, fire lily, moonpetal, nightshade, duskbell, hibiscus, bird of paradise, desert sage, starflower and voidbloom (which craft into dyes), and autumn fern, frost fern, ash grass, glimmercap, horsetail, royal fern, cattail, marsh grass, dune grass, inkcap and violet puffball.

In the world, each realm grows its grand trees, giants and smaller trees from its own wood and leaves, scatters its flowers and plants, piles its stone into boulders and spires, patches its soil into the ground and lines its caves with it. Autumn, swamp and palm trees in the Heartland use maple, cypress and palm too, and every End and Nether biome gets starstone or ashstone outcrops. All told, 486 biomes use the new blocks.

## A bigger world

**Wider biomes and landmasses.** The mod replaces vanilla's `temperature`, `vegetation`, `continentalness` and `erosion` noises with the ones vanilla uses for the Large Biomes preset, so every biome and every continent, ocean and plain is four times as wide. Rivers and mountain ridges come from the weirdness noise, which is left alone, so they keep vanilla's spacing. TerraBlender's regions are zoomed twice more to match (`LayeredNoiseUtilMixin`), so a realm or Heartland region still holds its biomes at the same proportions instead of carving each big biome into pieces. The Nether reads the same temperature and vegetation noises, so its biomes grow the same way.

**A 134 million block world.** Vanilla stops at ±30,000,000 because every block position is packed into one 64-bit number with 26 bits each for X and Z. The mod packs them with 27 bits (`BlockPosMixin`), and chunk sections with 23 instead of 22 (`SectionPosMixin`), which reaches ±67,108,864, and sets the edge of the world at ±67,000,000 (`WorldSize`). The world border of a new world is 133,999,968 blocks across, and every place vanilla checks the old 30 million limit uses the new one: block placement, entity positions, player movement, light, `/tp`, `/forceload`, `/worldborder` and `max-world-size`.

What that costs:

- **Install it on both sides.** Block positions travel over the network in the new packing, so a vanilla game would read them wrong. A server with the mod turns away players without it, and a player with the mod can only join servers that also run it; either way the disconnect screen says why.
- **Height is capped at -496 to 495.** The extra bits for X and Z come out of Y, which drops from 12 bits to 10. Vanilla's dimensions fit easily (the overworld is -64 to 319), but a datapack or mod that makes a dimension taller than that won't load. Only a dimension's own height is held to that (`DimensionTypeMixin`). Worldgen that just uses heights as numbers, like terrain gradients or feature height ranges, keeps vanilla's limits, so terrain mods such as Tectonic still load. Players and other entities can still fly higher, though some effects and sounds up there play at the wrong height.
- **Other mods that pack positions themselves** (some performance, lighting and rendering mods, or LOD mods like Distant Horizons) may not expect the new layout. Nothing beyond vanilla, Fabric API and TerraBlender has been tested with it.
- **Use a new world.** Chunks generated before won't line up with the new terrain and biome scale. Worlds whose border and `max-world-size` are still at vanilla's default are moved up to the new size when they load; a border you set yourself is left as it is.
- **Far out, things wobble.** Rendering precision drops with distance from the centre, as it does near vanilla's edge, and it's worse at 67 million.

## Where things live

- `src/main/resources/data/mysticalterrain/worldgen/`: the biomes, and the placed features they use (grouped per realm, with `shared/` for ones several realms use and `heartland/` for the vanilla-style variants, sorted by trait).
- `src/main/resources/data/mysticalterrain/terrablender/`: `regions.json` (which vanilla biome each region replaces, with up to two biomes per slot, plus where cave biomes go; the `weight` fields set how common each region is) and `surface_rules.json` (ground blocks). The mod reads these at startup.
- `src/main/resources/data/minecraft/tags/worldgen/biome/`: biome tags. Each biome joins the same vanilla tags as the biome it replaces.
- `src/main/java/.../biome/`: loads the two TerraBlender files and registers the regions.
- `src/main/resources/data/mysticalterrain/worldgen/configured_feature/grand_tree/`: what each grand tree looks like (shape, size, blocks and, for grown species, their genes). `placed_feature/grand_tree/` says how often it grows, `structure/` and `structure_set/` hold the landmarks and colossi, and `grand_trees.json` lists the trees of each biome.
- `src/main/java/.../tree/`: the grand tree generator. It builds a tree's trunk, branches, roots and foliage as shapes, then turns those into blocks one chunk at a time, which is what lets landmarks span many chunks. `Colonizer` grows the branches of the grown species and `Growth` holds their genes.
- `src/main/java/.../feature/`: the grand tree feature and landmark structure, the `grand_tree_count` placement that reads the rates, plus the colossal tree, spire and boulder feature types.
- `src/main/java/.../biome/MysticalTrees.java`: adds each biome's trees from `grand_trees.json`, vanilla biomes included, and takes vanilla's trees out of vanilla's biomes and ours.
- `src/main/resources/data/mysticalterrain/worldgen/placed_feature/end/` and `nether/`: everything the End and Nether biomes grow, including their own trees. `configured_feature/formation/` holds the End and Nether structures, placed by `structure/` and `structure_set/` like the landmark trees.
- `src/main/resources/data/mysticalterrain/terrablender/`: also `nether_region.json` (where each Nether biome sits in the climate), `nether_surface_rules.json` (their floors) and `end_biomes.json` (the zone and weight of each End biome).
- `src/main/resources/data/minecraft/worldgen/noise/`: the four climate noises, set to vanilla's Large Biomes values.
- `src/main/java/.../mixin/` and `WorldSize.java`: the bigger world. `BlockPosMixin` and `SectionPosMixin` change the packing, `DimensionTypeMixin` keeps dimensions inside the height that packs, the rest move vanilla's 30 million limits and world border defaults, and `LayeredNoiseUtilMixin` widens TerraBlender's regions. `WorldSizeCheck` and the client entrypoint turn away connections where only one side has the mod.
- `src/main/java/.../block/`: the new blocks. `MysticalBlocks` registers them in creative-tab order along with their flammability, stripping and the tab; `PlantBlock` is the grasses and small mushrooms.
- `src/main/resources/assets/mysticalterrain/textures/block/`: the block textures, each with `_n` and `_s` maps for shaders.
- `src/main/java/.../formation/`: the generator behind the crystals, craters, fossils, ruins, shrines, gates, floating islands, geodes, stars and reefs. Like the trees, each is built from shapes and turned into blocks one chunk at a time. `feature/` has the formation feature and structure, and the ground cover feature that gives End biomes their surface.

## Building

```
./gradlew build
```

The jar ends up in `build/libs/`. Fabric API and TerraBlender (Fabric) are required at runtime, and the mod has to be on the server and every client. Start a new world to get the new biomes, scale and size.

